ACPI_PANASONIC(4)        BSD Kernel Interfaces Manual        ACPI_PANASONIC(4)

NNAAMMEE
     aaccppii__ppaannaassoonniicc — ACPI hotkey driver for Panasonic laptops

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     To compile this driver into the kernel, place the following line in your
     kernel configuration file:

           ddeevviiccee aaccppii__ppaannaassoonniicc

     Alternatively, to load the driver as a module at boot time, place the
     following line in loader.conf(5):

           acpi_panasonic_load="YES"

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The aaccppii__ppaannaassoonniicc driver enables such hotkey facilities of various Pana‐
     sonic laptops as changing LCD brightness, controlling mixer volumes, en‐
     tering sleep or suspended state and so on.  On the following models it is
     reported to work: Let's note (or Toughbook, outside Japan) CF-R1N, CF-R2A
     and CF-R3.  It may also work on other models as well.

     The driver consists of three functionalities.  The first is to detect
     hotkey events and take corresponding actions, which include changing LCD
     luminance and speaker mute state.  The second role is to notify occur‐
     rences of the event by way of devctl(4) and eventually to devd(8).  The
     third and last is to provide a way to adjust LCD brightness and sound
     mute state via sysctl(8).

   HHoottkkeeyyss
     There are 9 hotkeys available on the supported hardware:

           FFnn++FF11       Make LCD backlight darker.
           FFnn++FF22       Make LCD backlight brighter.
           FFnn++FF33       Switch video output between LCD and CRT.  Not supported
                       by the aaccppii__ppaannaassoonniicc driver.
           FFnn++FF44       Toggle muting the speaker.
           FFnn++FF55       Turn the mixer volume down.
           FFnn++FF66       Turn the mixer volume up.
           FFnn++FF77       Enter suspend-to-RAM state.
           FFnn++FF99       Show battery status.
           FFnn++FF1100      Enter suspend-to-disk state.

     Actions are automatically taken within the driver for FFnn++FF11, FFnn++FF22 and
     FFnn++FF44.  For the other events such as mixer control and showing battery
     status, devd(8) should take the role as described below.

   devd(8) Events
     When notified to devd(8), the hotkey event provides the following infor‐
     mation:

           system      "ACPI"
           subsystem   "Panasonic"
           type        The source of the event in ACPI namespace.  The value
                       depends on the model but typically "\_SB_.HKEY".
           notify      Event code (see below).

     Event codes to be generated are assigned as follows:

           0x81-0x86, 0x89
                       FFnn++FF<<nn>> pressed.  0x81 corresponds to FFnn++FF11, 0x82 cor‐
                       responds to FFnn++FF22, and so on.

           0x01-0x07, 0x09, 0x1a
                       FFnn++FF<<nn>> released.  0x01 corresponds to FFnn++FF11, 0x02 cor‐
                       responds to FFnn++FF22, and so on.

